%I A399549 #55 Sep 17 2026 18:55:05
%S A399549 1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,3,2,1,1,5,9,7,1,1,7,18,20,9,3,1,1,9,33,66,84,73,38,
%T A399549 9,1,1,12,63,190,359,429,311,128,30,5,1,1,15,99,377,916,1497,1727,
%U A399549 1511,1083,631,267,72,12,1,1,18,147,726,2445,6008,11209,16137,17821,14764,8904,3791,1105,210,25,2
%N A399549 Irregular triangle T(n, k) read by rows. T(n, k) gives the coefficient [t^k] P_{n}(t) from the Somos-4 sequence of polynomials P_{0}(t) = P_{1}(t) = P_{2}(t) = P_{3}(t)=1; for n >= 4, P_{n}(t) = (P_{n-1}(t) * P_{n-3}(t) + t*P_{n-2}(t)^2) / P_{n-4}(t).
%C A399549 The length of row n>9 is given by length(n) = 2*length(n-1) - length(n-2) + length(n-7) - 2*length(n-8) + length(n-9), with initial length n=0...9 : {1,1,1,1,2,2,4,5,7,9};
%C A399549 _Michael Somos_ defined the homogenized Somos polynomials as x*y*z*(S(n-1)*S(n-3) + t*S(n-2)^2)/S(n-4), with initial conditions S(0) = x, S(1) = S(2) = 1, S(3) = y. This triangle gives the coefficients of a less general version.
%C A399549 The zeros of these polynomials are interesting. All Somos sequences obtained by inserting rational values of t correspond to point multiplication of some point under group law of a particular elliptic curve (for details see also A378125), where the Somos sequence can be interpreted as the evaluation of elliptic division polynomials. Zeros of elliptic division polynomials correspond directly to the points of finite order (torsion points) on an elliptic curve. As all polynomial coefficients are strictly positive, we find that also for non-integer t > 0 all orbits are not from torsion points. If we set t = -1, the row polynomials P_n(t) will evaluate into a sequence that is periodic with period: [1,1,1,1,0,-1,-1,-1,-1,0], this is explained by the torsion point orbit of (0, 0) on the elliptic curve y^2 + y = x^3 - x^2 (Cremona label 11a3).
%C A399549 The parameter t has the same meaning like n in A377441, however the generating function used in A377441 looks different. This is because A377441 considers Somos sequences that are shifted by one term. If we consider the generalized initial conditions of our sequence by setting P_3(t) = m, then we obtain the generalized generating function: ((m^2*x + m*t*x^2 - m*t*x - (m + 2)*t*x + m*x - m - t*x + 2*t - 3*x + 1) - sqrt((m^2*(-x) - m*t*x^2 + m*t*x + (m + 2)*t*x - m*x + m + t*x - 2*t + 3*x - 1)^2 - 4*(-m*t*x - t*x + t - x + 1)*(-(m^2*(x - 1)*x) + m*t*x^2 + (-m - 2)*t*x + 2*m*x - m + t - 2*x + 1)))/(2*(-(m^2*(x - 1)*x) + m*t*x^2 + (-m - 2)*t*x + 2*m*x - m + t - 2*x + 1)). If we substitute m = t+1 it resolves into the generating function given by A377441.
%H A399549 Thomas Scheuerle, <a href="/A399549/a399549_5.pdf">Explanation how Somos-4 sequences and generating functions relate to each other</a>.
%H A399549 Michael Somos, <a href="https://grail.eecs.csuohio.edu/~somos/somospol.html">Somos Polynomials</a>.
%F A399549 Let c_k(t) = [x^k] (((t*(x^2 - 3*x + 1) - 2*t*x + t - x + 1) - sqrt((-t*(x^2 - 3*x + 1) + 2*t*x - t + x - 1)^2 - 4*(-2*t*x + t - x + 1)*(t*(x^2 - 3*x + 1) + (1 - x)*x)))/(2*(t*(x^2 - 3*x + 1) + (1 - x)*x))). Let H(c_n(t)) denote the Hankel transform of the polynomial sequence c_0(t), c_1(t), c_2(t), ..., then P_n(t) = H(c_n(t)) and T(n, k) = [t^k]P_n(t).
%F A399549 T(n, 1) = A001840(n-3) for n > 2.
%e A399549 Triangle T(n, k) starts:
%e A399549   [ 0] 1
%e A399549   [ 1] 1
%e A399549   [ 2] 1
%e A399549   [ 3] 1
%e A399549   [ 4] 1,  1
%e A399549   [ 5] 1,  2
%e A399549   [ 6] 1,  3,  2,   1
%e A399549   [ 7] 1,  5,  9,   7,   1
%e A399549   [ 8] 1,  7, 18,  20,   9,   3,   1
%e A399549   [ 9] 1,  9, 33,  66,  84,  73,  38,   9,  1
%e A399549   [10] 1, 12, 63, 190, 359, 429, 311, 128, 30, 5, 1
%o A399549 (PARI)
%o A399549 Hankel(v) = vector((#v+1)\2, n, matdet(matrix(n, n, i, j, v[i+j-1])));
%o A399549 Row(n) = my(y=('y+O(y^(2*n+1))),t='t); Vecrev(Hankel(Vec(((t*(y^2 - 3*y + 1) - 2*t*y + t - y + 1) - sqrt((-t*(y^2 - 3*y + 1) + 2*t*y - t + y - 1)^2 - 4*(-2*t*y + t - y + 1)*(t*(y^2 - 3*y + 1) + (1 - y)*y)))/(2*(t*(y^2 - 3*y + 1) + (1 - y)*y))))[n+1]);
%Y A399549 Cf. A006720 (row sums), A377441, A377442, A378125.
%Y A399549 Cf. A001840.
